    "question": {
      ":question/id": "03037866-9e99-5a94-acdf-a5e7b89db4df",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)$:\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\left(\\frac{10}{3}\\right)\\cdot y=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{d}{dx}(yI(x)) = I(x)Q(x)$, where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{10}{3}$ and $Q(x)=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =e^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & =e^{\\int \\left(\\frac{10}{3}\\right)\\,dx} \\\\  & =e^{\\frac{10 x}{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)=e^{\\frac{10 x}{3}}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(e^{\\frac{10 x}{3}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\left(\\frac{10}{3}\\right)\\cdot y\\right) & =\\left(e^{\\frac{10 x}{3}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right) \\\\ \\left(e^{\\frac{10 x}{3}}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}+\\left(\\frac{10}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(e^{\\frac{10 x}{3}}\\right)\\cdot y & =\\left(e^{\\frac{10 x}{3}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(y\\cdot \\left(e^{\\frac{10 x}{3}}\\right)\\right) & =\\left(e^{\\frac{10 x}{3}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
